"Shows point estimates with explicitly defined intervals so location and uncertainty can be compared together.\n\nUse when: A set of estimates needs a concise view of their associated intervals. Interval bounds have a documented statistical or descriptive meaning.\n\nAvoid when: The distribution is strongly irregular and a single interval would hide important structure.\n\nVisual grammar: Point position encodes the estimate. Whisker endpoints encode lower and upper bounds in the same measurement units.\n\nBuild: Calculate the estimate and interval using a named method and level. Place each estimate on a category row with a common quantitative axis. Draw thin whiskers and caps, labeling the interval meaning near the title.\n\nCheck: Check lower bound ≤ estimate ≤ upper bound when the chosen interval definition requires it. Verify confidence, prediction, standard error, and standard deviation are not interchanged. State sample sizes and any weighting or transformation.\n\nPitfalls: Overlapping confidence intervals do not directly determine the significance of a difference. Keep whiskers within their rows and away from labels; use a distribution display for complex uncertainty.\n\nExample: Compare synthetic mean service times across four teams with explicitly labeled 95% bootstrap confidence intervals.\n\nAlternatives: Forest plot, Confidence band, Gradient interval plot."
